WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.980
New helium measurements are confirming the exact mix forged in the universe's first minutes. This is Fascinating Frontiers, Episode 221. Astronomers still have to peel helium forged in the Big Bang apart from helium later cooked inside stars, according to Fizz.org. By mass, that first inventory ran roughly three parts hydrogen against one part helium. Everything heavier arrived later, either through fusion and stars or in supernovae. Those heavier species stay so scarce that astronomers

00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:32.900
astronomers still bundle them under one label, Metals.

00:00:33.420 --> 00:00:38.360
The primordial share is read from spectral lines in the oldest gas clouds, which still

00:00:38.360 --> 00:00:40.220
hold the original abundance ratios.

00:00:40.840 --> 00:00:46.200
After stellar helium added over cosmic time is subtracted, the leftover fraction continues

00:00:46.200 --> 00:00:49.700
to line up with standard Big Bang nucleosynthesis calculations.

00:00:50.460 --> 00:00:54.440
One second after the Big Bang, take a volume the size of a large city block.

00:00:54.980 --> 00:00:59.540
That single block already held enough energy to form every atom now in the observable

00:00:59.540 --> 00:01:04.480
universe. The energy for the entire visible inventory sat inside a patch you could walk

00:01:04.480 --> 00:01:09.960
across. Then the clock ran only two minutes more. In those two minutes the temperature fell

00:01:09.960 --> 00:01:15.340
from billions of degrees to roughly one billion. Protons and neutrons fused into the first

00:01:15.340 --> 00:01:20.620
nuclei while the cosmos was still hot enough to allow it. Then the universe cooled too far

00:01:20.620 --> 00:01:26.040
for further reactions, and the nuclear work of the beginning stopped. Only about one helium

00:01:26.040 --> 00:01:33.280
nucleus formed for every 12 hydrogen nuclei. That freeze left a 75 to 25 percent mix. The mix

00:01:33.280 --> 00:01:38.420
still measured today. The ratio depends on the exact expansion rate in those first moments.

00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:44.180
It also depends on the number of neutrino species present then. Nobody has explained why the

00:01:44.180 --> 00:01:49.420
measured values sit so precisely on the theoretical curve, rather than missing it by even a few

00:01:49.420 --> 00:01:55.440
percent. If a mismatch appears in future data, it would force a revision of the standard model itself.

00:01:56.040 --> 00:02:00.620
Observations continue to test whether additional physics operated during those first minutes.

00:02:01.280 --> 00:02:06.500
New simulations find the Sun's mass loss could destabilize some outer planets in roughly one

00:02:06.500 --> 00:02:12.740
billion years rather than a quintillion, Fizz.org reports. That revision shortens earlier estimates

00:02:12.740 --> 00:02:18.460
by many orders of magnitude for the most distant worlds. The new timeline appears in the astrophysical

00:02:18.460 --> 00:02:23.780
journal letters, after orbital evolution was recalculated through the Sun's red giant phase.

00:02:23.780 --> 00:02:28.880
The runs follow gradual mass shedding as it weakens gravitational binding for planets

00:02:28.880 --> 00:02:30.020
at large distances.

00:02:30.620 --> 00:02:35.580
Small perturbations that once needed immense spans of time to grow now produce instability

00:02:35.580 --> 00:02:37.820
on far shorter cosmic time scales.

00:02:38.300 --> 00:02:43.460
The study concentrates on the outer solar system, where those effects build up most noticeably.

00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:46.740
Mass loss accelerates once the sun is a red giant.

00:02:47.440 --> 00:02:51.120
Distant orbits remain sensitive to even modest changes in the central mass.

00:02:51.120 --> 00:02:58.280
One open question is whether similar mass loss could unbind planets around other stars at different evolutionary stages.

00:02:58.840 --> 00:03:04.700
A wide angle composite from Chile's Atacama Desert sets a stairway sculpture against the rising Milky Way,

00:03:05.140 --> 00:03:08.900
according to the National Aeronautics and Space Administration, or NASA.

00:03:09.580 --> 00:03:16.300
Photographer Julian Luton made the frame in July 2025, so the staircase would line up with the near vertical band.

00:03:16.300 --> 00:03:21.080
The picture takes in the dark lane of the Milky Way and a band of zodiacal light that

00:03:21.080 --> 00:03:21.920
contains speaker.

00:03:22.640 --> 00:03:27.300
Green and red air glow sits near the horizon, and the small Magellanic cloud appears

00:03:27.300 --> 00:03:28.160
at the top right.

00:03:28.800 --> 00:03:33.760
The Andes Mountains, Lycanca Boer volcano, and lights from San Pedro de Atacama share

00:03:33.760 --> 00:03:34.780
that same horizon.

00:03:35.340 --> 00:03:40.080
The sculpture sits just in front of the dense band, while the zodiacal light climbs on

00:03:40.080 --> 00:03:40.460
the left.

00:03:40.460 --> 00:03:45.300
The image is the astronomy picture of the day for October 13th, 2026.

00:03:46.080 --> 00:03:50.860
NASA is asking for ideas to stop static electricity from building on space suits and equipment

00:03:50.860 --> 00:03:53.960
during lunar surface work, space.com reports.

00:03:54.660 --> 00:03:59.340
The moon's dry, dusty setting lets charge pile up on crews, who could then discharge

00:03:59.340 --> 00:04:01.040
into a spacecraft or a tool.

00:04:01.600 --> 00:04:06.160
The agency flagged the risk in a call for mitigation concepts ahead of planned, Artemis

00:04:06.160 --> 00:04:06.720
landings.

00:04:06.720 --> 00:04:11.480
Dry Regolith transfers charge readily because there is no atmospheric moisture.

00:04:12.140 --> 00:04:16.780
An astronaut crossing the surface can behave like a walking capacitor and deliver an unexpected

00:04:16.780 --> 00:04:17.860
shock to hardware.

00:04:18.460 --> 00:04:22.740
The request for proposals covers both suit materials and operational procedures.

00:04:23.440 --> 00:04:28.480
NASA's Jet Propulsion Laboratory is offering limited public tours on select dates this month.

00:04:29.040 --> 00:04:30.600
The Pasadena Star News reports.

00:04:31.380 --> 00:04:35.860
Visitors will see mission control rooms and spacecraft assembly areas normally restricted

00:04:35.860 --> 00:04:37.480
to staff and invited guests.

00:04:38.240 --> 00:04:41.380
The laboratory is treating the openings as a special outreach effort.

00:04:41.920 --> 00:04:45.900
The tours give direct views of current mission operations and hardware integration.

00:04:46.580 --> 00:04:49.520
Access stays tightly controlled even during the open periods.

00:04:50.140 --> 00:04:54.760
The aim is to connect visitors with the laboratory's planetary and astrophysics projects.

00:04:55.300 --> 00:05:00.260
Science City in Gujarat is running World Space Week sessions on model rocketry, telescope

00:05:00.260 --> 00:05:03.760
building, and talks about how satellites show up in daily life.

00:05:03.760 --> 00:05:09.780
The Ahmadabad Mirror reports, between 1,000 and 1,200 students are expected in the hands-on

00:05:09.780 --> 00:05:10.380
sessions.

00:05:10.840 --> 00:05:15.920
A scientist's circle will discuss how space technology supports communication, weather forecasting

00:05:15.920 --> 00:05:16.940
and navigation.

00:05:17.640 --> 00:05:21.420
Workshops walk through rocket design principles and propulsion fundamentals.

00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:25.080
A separate activity lets participants build simple telescopes.

00:05:25.660 --> 00:05:30.620
The events run through the week, with an emphasis on practical demonstrations of space applications.

00:05:30.620 --> 00:05:36.820
Bengaluru is marking 70 years since Sputnik, the world's first satellite, the Hindustan Times

00:05:36.820 --> 00:05:37.180
reports.

00:05:37.840 --> 00:05:41.920
The city has been headquarters of India's space program since the 1970s.

00:05:42.300 --> 00:05:47.620
The account traces Indian space research from ionosphere studies in the 1920s, through

00:05:47.620 --> 00:05:50.840
institutes founded by Homi Baba and Vikram Sarabai.

00:05:51.480 --> 00:05:56.100
S.K. Mitra ran pioneering ionosphere experiments in the 1920s.

00:05:56.100 --> 00:06:00.540
Aignad-Saha and CV-Raman advanced related principles in the 1930s.

00:06:01.120 --> 00:06:07.040
Baba established the Tata Institute of Fundamental Research in 1945, and Sarabai founded the

00:06:07.040 --> 00:06:09.560
Physical Research Laboratory in 1947.

00:06:10.340 --> 00:06:15.720
The 1967 outer space treaty entered into force in the same October window now observed as

00:06:15.720 --> 00:06:16.680
World Space Week.

00:06:17.360 --> 00:06:21.680
Jet propulsion laboratories limited tour dates this month will show whether those normally

00:06:21.680 --> 00:06:25.240
closed control rooms and assembly floors actually open as advertised.

